          %title The fundamental theorem of calculus
          %h1#the_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus The fundamental theorem of calculus
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#statements"} Statements
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#theorems_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus"} Theorems (Fundamental Theorem of Calculus)
              %li
                %a{:href => "#proofs"} Proofs
              %li
                %a{:href => "#integral_formula_for_antiderivative"} Integral formula for antiderivative
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The fundamental theorem of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+calculus"} infinitesimal calculus
            (FTC) states that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antiderivatives"} antiderivatives
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indefinite+integrals"} indefinite integrals
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            (typically a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            in the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definite+integral"} definite integral
            and sometimes called the
            %em semidefinite
            integral, that is really in play here.
          %p The FTC is usually split into two parts. The first part (sometimes called the second part) states that every indefinite integral is an antiderivative. The second part (sometimes called the first part) states that every antiderivative is an indefinite integral. Somewhere in here (usually in the first part) we also want to state that such antiderivatives and indefinite integrals actually exist. Their uniqueness (such as it is) may also be included.
          %p
            The second part is a low-dimensional version of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stokes+theorem"} Stokes theorem
          %p
            The FTC may or may not actually be true, depending on what class of functions is considered and what notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            are used.
          %h2#statements Statements
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %strong antiderivative
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a solution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F'(x) = f(x) .
          %p
            Also recall (or learn) than an
            %strong indefinite integral
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is any function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi a
                    %mi x
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi t
                  %mo +
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x) = \int_a^x f(t) \,\mathrm{d}t + C
          %p
            for a real constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          #FTC.un_theorem
            %h6#theorems_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus Theorems (Fundamental Theorem of Calculus)
            %p
              Suppose that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              are appropriate functions, and use an appropriate notion of derivative and integral. Then:
            %ol
              %li
                First part: If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is an indefinite integral of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is an antiderivative of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %li
                Second part: If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is an antiderivative of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is an indefinite integral of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %li
                Existence:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                has an indefinite integral / antiderivative.
              %li
                Uniqueness: If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_1
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
                are antiderivatives of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_1 - F_2
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+function"} constant function
          %p This holds under any of the following conditions:
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is
              = succeed "," do
